      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village’s name comes from the Tanana word “Soł Chaget,” meaning “the mouth of Salcha,” and the U.S. Geological Survey called it “Salchaket” in 1898.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salcha%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Salcha sits on the Salcha River, a popular fishing destination, and is also near the Tanana River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salcha%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The community lies within the Salcha Seismic Zone, an area of notable seismic activity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salcha%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "other"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Salcha experiences a dry‑winter continental subarctic climate (Köppen Dwc), with long, cold winters and short, mild summers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
